Subject: OVN Issue – Missing SNAT Rules for EgressIP on OpenShift Cluster OCP-CBK-PRD-2 (Recurring)

We are currently experiencing an issue with OVN affecting two of our OpenShift clusters, both running version 4.18.19. Worker nodes on these clusters are distributed across two sites: VEGA and SIRIUS.

This case focuses on cluster OCP-CBK-PRD-2, which has encountered this issue twice in the past few days.

Cluster Topology

The cluster consists of 3 master nodes and 4 worker nodes:

Worker1 and Worker3 — VEGA Datacenter
Worker2 and Worker4 — SIRIUS Datacenter

Due to a known EgressIP assignment bug, we currently restrict EgressIP hosting to Worker1 and Worker2 only, by limiting the egress-assignable labels to these two nodes.

EgressIP Configuration

EgressIP is used across all client namespaces on this cluster, with a dedicated EgressIP per application (each covering one or more namespaces). Each EgressIP resource is configured with two IP addresses — one per datacenter.

Under normal operation, every pod in a client namespace should have two SNAT rules in the OVN database, corresponding to the two IPs defined in the EgressIP resource.

Issue Description

Intermittently, the cluster begins creating pods with only a single SNAT rule instead of two. From that point forward, any newly created or restarted pod is provisioned without the second SNAT rule.

In this occurrence on OCP-CBK-PRD-2, all newly created/restarted pods were missing the SNAT rule for the SIRIUS IP. As a result, egress traffic from affected pods was blocked entirely, causing a significant client-facing impact due to loss of pod-level connectivity.

Remediation Steps Taken

To resolve the issue, we performed the following actions:

Rolled out the OVN control-plane
Rolled out the OVN kube-node components
Migrated all SIRIUS EgressIP assignments from Worker2 to Worker4 by updating the OVN labels
Rolled out client workloads in select namespaces to restore correct pod configuration

Additional Notes

No errors were observed in the OVN pod logs (control-plane or ovn-kube-node) during the incident.

We will attach the must-gather for the cluster. Please note that we had to restart the entire OVN layer to restore service, which may affect the diagnostic data captured.
